From 463b314ee6d72649cc8afe11395e99315c6980b9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 04:46:59 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 07/10] chore: the prototype data layer is gone
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Five routes stopped rendering fiction. Content listed twelve invented
pieces with captions, objectives and versions; strategy rendered ten
chapters with an approval panel beside them, as if the client could
approve; results showed numbers with readings attached to them; meetings
had dates, times and links; files had sizes. None of it existed. Three of
those pages already had the right empty-state copy behind a branch almost
never taken — now that copy is the page.

The routes stay. Deep links are how most clients arrive, and a URL that
existed and starts answering 404 breaks every link already shared. They
leave the navigation, which is a different thing: the product stops
advertising empty rooms while the addresses keep working.

The project page stopped being a second home. It repeated the journey,
repeated a delivery date with no column anywhere, and listed past meetings
from a table that does not exist. It now owns the full journey with each
stage's summary, while the home shows three stages and a pointer.

The mock file, the data layer that held it, and the four components that
only existed to render its shapes are deleted. Those shapes were the
prototype's, not the database's — previewTone, sizeLabel and versionCount
are columns nowhere — and keeping them as reference would invite phase 9
or 10 to fit a real schema into an invented form. That is precisely what
phase 7 avoided by discarding the prototype's onboarding section and
rewriting it from the columns. Git and the phase patches keep the history.

Two guards from earlier phases got stronger rather than deleted: "only the
data layer imports mocks" became "nobody imports them", and "the onboarding
mock is gone" became "the mocks directory does not exist".

The fictional tenants stay in the seed and in the RLS fixtures. Without two
distinct tenants there is no isolation suite; what is forbidden is
client-facing hard-coding under src.

